   242	## 9. Full weak-preservation theorem for uniformly curved losses
   243	
   244	### Theorem 9 (all fixed dimensions; finite-variance errors, including atoms)
   245	
   246	Let p be fixed, let (x_i) be one deterministic nested design with S_{n_0}>0 for some n_0, and let rho be a fixed C^2 function satisfying
   247	
   248	0 < m <= rho''(r) <= M < infinity for every real r.
   249	
   250	Let the errors be i.i.d. with finite variance sigma^2 (their mean need not be zero). Then the original and repeated-design M-minimizers exist uniquely for n>=n_0, and **original weak consistency implies repeated-design weak consistency**, in every dimension p. No density, score-centering E rho'(epsilon)=0, or Fisher-identification assumption is needed. This theorem is about weak consistency and therefore does not depend on the strong coupling R/S/I.
   251	
   252	More precisely, let theta_n be the unique minimizer of the deterministic population criterion E F_n. Then
   253	
   254	E||beta_hat_n-theta_n||^2
   255	 <= M^2 sigma^2 p / [m^2 lambda_min(S_n)],                         (9.1)
   256	
   257	E||beta_tilde_{2n}-theta_n||^2
   258	 <= M^2 sigma^2 p / [2 m^2 lambda_min(S_n)].                      (9.2)
   259	
   260	If the error law is nondegenerate, original weak consistency forces lambda_min(S_n)->infinity. Consequently, in this class original weak consistency is equivalent to the conjunction of Gram divergence and theta_n->beta_0. The latter deterministic minimizer is unchanged by exact design replication.
   261	
   262	**Proof of existence and notation.** Put psi=rho'. The curvature bounds imply that psi is M-Lipschitz and has at most linear growth, while rho has at most quadratic growth and a positive quadratic lower bound up to an affine function. Thus the finite sample criterion and its expectation are finite, coercive, and strictly convex whenever S_n>0. They have unique minimizers, their gradients exist, and differentiation of the finite expected sum is justified by finite second moments. This also makes the sample minimizer a measurable, continuous function of the response vector.
   263	
   264	**Step 1: consistency forces Gram divergence without a density assumption.** This step only requires a nondegenerate error law, not moments. Suppose Gram divergence fails. Since S_n increases, S_n^{-1} decreases in the positive-semidefinite order for n>=n_0 and converges to a positive-semidefinite matrix B. Failure of lambda_min(S_n)->infinity implies B is nonzero. The first n_0 design vectors span R^p, so there is a fixed i<=n_0 with
   265	
   266	x_i'B x_i = c > 0.
   267	
   268	Therefore x_i'S_n^{-1}x_i>=c for every n>=n_0. Hold every response except y_i fixed. The unique sample minimizer b(y_i) is continuously differentiable by the implicit function theorem, since its Hessian
   269	
   270	H = sum_{j<=n} rho''(y_j-x_j'b) x_j x_j'
   271	
   272	satisfies mS_n<=H<=MS_n and is invertible. Differentiating the first-order equation gives
   273	
   274	db/dy_i = H^{-1} rho''(y_i-x_i'b) x_i.
   275	
   276	Thus the fitted value g_n(y_i)=x_i'b(y_i) has, for every real y_i and every choice of the other responses,
   277	
   278	g_n'(y_i)
   279	 = rho''(y_i-x_i'b) x_i'H^{-1}x_i
   280	 >= (m/M) x_i'S_n^{-1}x_i
   281	 >= alpha := (m/M)c > 0.                                      (9.3)
   282	
   283	Nondegeneracy gives real a<b with q_-=P(epsilon_i<=a)>0 and q_+=P(epsilon_i>=b)>0. Conditional on all other responses, the fitted values arising from these two events are separated by at least alpha(b-a), since y_i=x_i'beta_0+epsilon_i and (9.3) holds everywhere. An interval of radius eta=alpha(b-a)/4 cannot intersect both ranges. Hence, uniformly over n and the other responses,
   284	
   285	P(|x_i'beta_hat_n-x_i'beta_0|>eta | other responses)
   286	 >= min(q_-,q_+) > 0.
   287	
   288	Independence ensures the conditional error law remains the original law. Averaging this inequality contradicts beta_hat_n->beta_0 in probability, because x_i is fixed and nonzero. This proves Gram divergence.
   289	
   290	**Step 2: concentration about the deterministic population minimizer.** At theta_n write
   291	
   292	Z_n = sum_{i<=n} x_i {psi(Y_i-x_i'theta_n)
   293	                         - E psi(Y_i-x_i'theta_n)}.
   294	
   295	The population first-order equation gives grad F_n(theta_n)=-Z_n. Put d=beta_hat_n-theta_n. Strong monotonicity of the sample gradient, with grad F_n(beta_hat_n)=0, yields
   296	
   297	m d'S_n d <= d' Z_n
   298	           <= ||S_n^{1/2}d|| ||S_n^{-1/2}Z_n||.
   299	
   300	Consequently ||S_n^{1/2}d||<=m^{-1}||S_n^{-1/2}Z_n||, including d=0. For any deterministic shift t and independent copies epsilon,epsilon',
   301	
   302	Var(psi(epsilon-t))
   303	 = (1/2) E[psi(epsilon-t)-psi(epsilon'-t)]^2
   304	 <= (M^2/2) E(epsilon-epsilon')^2
   305	 = M^2 sigma^2.
   306	
   307	Independence and the trace identity sum_i x_i'S_n^{-1}x_i=p now give
   308	
   309	E||S_n^{-1/2}Z_n||^2
   310	 = sum_i Var(psi(Y_i-x_i'theta_n)) x_i'S_n^{-1}x_i
   311	 <= M^2 sigma^2 p.
   312	
